// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type
// THE SHADOW TEST, PINNED AT THE SPELLING WHERE IT DECIDES SOMETHING — the local-shadow twin
// of `pin-global-binding-both-scopes.vl`'s leg 5.
//
// D703 makes `monoGlobalLetOf` answer from INSIDE a function, where it used to decline on
// `fnIx >= 0` outright. The decline is now the shadow test itself: a parameter of that name,
// or a `let`/`const` of it anywhere in the body. This file is the second half — an
// un-annotated LOCAL `sh` over a module-scope `const sh: {r: i32[]}[]`, a spelling
// `monoAnnPinName` DOES claim, so if the global scan ran ahead of the shadow test the
// instance would declare a `(ref $rlWrap)` parameter over an i32 caller.
//
// IT MUST STAY THIS REJECT, byte for byte with the parent, and it does. The local arm above
// declines (an un-annotated `const sh = 7` recurses into its init and gets the `"i32"`
// catch-all, which is not adopted), the shadow test then keeps the global out, and the
// cascade below reaches `exprStruct`/`structIndexOfExpr` — which resolve the IDENT through
// the module table, because `buildLocals` is post-mono, and answer about the GLOBAL.
//
// THAT LAST SENTENCE IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND IT IS FILED (D705, `silent-class-inventory`).
// The same program with a `string` or a struct global is `vl check` rc 0 and INVALID WASM on
// the parent as well as here — the rep classifiers are blind to shadowing mid-mono, which is
// not something a pin arm can fix. This file pins only what the pin arm owns: the shadowed
// name must not take the GLOBAL's annotation.
function idg<T>(x: T): T { return x }
const sh: {r: i32[]}[] = [{ r: [1] }]
function leg() {
  const sh = 7
  print(idg(sh))
}
leg()
print(sh.length)
